## Why This Is Asked
The question probes real-world debugging of latency on a critical path involving feature-flags, caching, and data sinks. It requires concrete instrumentation, trade-offs, and verification strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flag evaluation latency
- Distributed caching
- Backpressure and circuit breakers
- Canary testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple in-process LRU cache for flags
class FlagCache {
  constructor(ttlMs) { this.ttl=ttlMs; this.map=new Map(); }
  get(k){ const v=this.map.get(k); if(!v) return null; if(Date.now()-v.ts>this.ttl) { this.map.delete(k); return null; } return v.val; }
  set(k,v){ this.map.set(k,{val:v,ts:Date.now()}); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-hot flags or dynamic TTLs?
- What are the risks of stale flags and how to mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
The question probes ability to guarantee audit completeness and ordering under load via transactional patterns and robust sinks.

## Key Concepts
- Transactional outbox pattern
- Exactly-once delivery with Kafka
- Idempotent sinks and deduplication
- End-to-end auditing and verification under load

## Code Example
```javascript
async function writeWithOutbox(db, data) {
  const tx = await db.begin();
  await tx.query("UPDATE users SET ... WHERE id = $1", [data.id]);
  await tx.query("INSERT INTO audit_outbox (tx_id, payload) VALUES ($1, $2)", [data.tx_id, JSON.stringify(data.audit)]);
  await tx.commit();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify no audit entries are dropped during a traffic spike?
- Which metrics indicate an outbox backlog or duplicate audits, and how would you address them?

